[38;2;124;124;124md(A₁,A₂) = ∫|1_{A₁} - 1_{A₂}|?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is the L¹ metric on indicator functions. The question is whether the set of[0m[38;2;124;124;124mall measurable subsets of [0,1] is totally bounded (has a countable ε-net for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mevery ε) in this metric.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think. The metric d(A₁,A₂) = ∫|1_{A₁} - 1_{A₂}| = m(A₁ △ A₂), the measure[0m[38;2;124;124;124mof the symmetric difference.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo we're asking: for every ε > 0, is there a countable family F of measurable [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msets such that every measurable set A ⊆ [0,1] is within ε (in symmetric [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdifference measure) of some set in F?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is YES. This is a classical result. The measurable sets form a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcomplete separable metric space under the symmetric difference metric [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(identifying sets that differ by a null set). The separability comes from the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfact that simple functions are dense in L¹, or more directly, the algebra [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgenerated by dyadic intervals (or intervals with rational endpoints) is dense.[0m

